artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a doped manganite modeled as an inhomogeneous two-phase medium in which, below a characteristic temperature scale, metallic ferromagnetic clusters coexist with an insulating (charge/orbital-ordered or paramagnetic) background. Let p(T) denote the temperature-dependent volume fraction of the metallic phase, with intrinsic resistivities ρ_M(T) for the metallic regions and ρ_I(T) for the insulating regions (e.g. with activated behavior ρ_I(T) ∝ exp(Δ/k_B T) for some gap Δ, and ρ_M(T) showing typical metallic temperature dependence). Assume a percolative transport scenario in which a macroscopic current flows when p(T) exceeds a percolation threshold p_c, and that the assignment of phases to regions of space is governed by an underlying random-field or competing-order free-energy model with parameters that can in principle be extracted from microscopic Hamiltonians for manganites.

Problem. Develop a quantitative theory of phase separation in this setting that yields a closed-form, analytically tractable formula ρ_dc(T) = F(T; p(T), ρ_M(T), ρ_I(T), p_c, …) for the temperature dependence of the d.c. resistivity in the percolative regime, such that:
(i) F is given by simple, explicit functions (not only by numerical resistor-network simulations on a lattice);
(ii) F reproduces the colossal magnetoresistance-type peak in ρ_dc(T) near the metal–insulator transition observed in manganites;
(iii) The parameters entering F are derivable from, or directly relatable to, a microscopic model of competing ferromagnetic-metallic and insulating phases.

State and justify such a predictive formula, or prove that within the percolative phase-separation framework no such universal closed-form expression can capture the observed ρ_dc(T) without resorting to full network simulations.

